 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 
-Cyclic projection turns one exponential into a finite differential basis.  A signed cycle gives the denominator $\Dfun_N$.  Reciprocal powers of this denominator form a one parameter semigroup, and their normalized coefficients form a sequence of binomial type.  The logarithmic derivative satisfies a higher Riccati equation.  Normalized exponential sections produce congruential reciprocal functions and ordinary Appell systems.  A scaled reciprocal coupling produces the theta polynomials.
-
-The finite algebra model shows that the differential cycles and addition laws come from one exponential in a quotient algebra.  The change of variable $w=z^N$ removes the repeated rotational copies of the zeros.  The reduced denominator has order $1/N$, a genus zero Hadamard product, and a trace class determinant model.  Its zero power sums are exactly the logarithmic coefficients that govern the transfer tangent and the reciprocal Bell polynomials.  Two adjacent exponentials determine every sufficiently large zero.  This gives a first displacement term, eventual simplicity, the exact zero counting constant, derivative growth, and meromorphic continuation of the symmetry grouped zero zeta.
-
-The common analytic chain is
-\[
-\begin{gathered}
-\text{cyclic projection}
-\longrightarrow
-\text{finite differential basis}
-\longrightarrow
-\text{reciprocal powers}
-\\
-\longrightarrow
-\text{logarithmic coefficients}
-\longrightarrow
-\text{zero power sums}
-\longrightarrow
-\text{zero zeta}.
-\end{gathered}
-\]
-Each step is exact under the hypotheses stated in the corresponding theorem.
